Physics-informed operator learning enables prediction across families of PDEs without paired solution-field supervision, yet conventional
representations often lack explicit and physically interpretable, task-dependent geometric structure. To address this gap, we introduce KAPI-ONet, a physics-informed operator network that represents solutions as continuous fields of task-adaptive Gaussian radial basis functions with interpretable centers, scales, orientations, and coefficients. A parent--child construction shares geometric transformations to reduce representation complexity, while a Gaussian-splat functional autoencoder (GSFAE) provides a structured interface for functional inputs. We establish theoretical properties of the ideal kernel representation, including approximation capacity and geometric-sharing error. Controlled ablations demonstrate the independent benefit of task-adaptive output geometry, while input-encoding comparisons clarify the complementary role and limitations of GSFAE. Experiments spanning forward and inverse PDE problems, parametric flows, and weakly supervised biomedical applications demonstrate the framework's versatility and accuracy. Additional convergence and computational analyses characterize its accuracy--efficiency trade-offs. These findings position task-adaptive Gaussian geometry as an interpretable and effective representation for physics-informed operator learning. Code and reproducibility materials are available at:
